Now I understand the structure. The key issue is in `decode_NXAST_RAW_ENCAP`:

```c
encap = ofpact_put_ENCAP(out);
...
ofp_prop = nae->props;
props_len = ntohs(nae->len) - offsetof(struct nx_action_encap, props);
n_props = 0;
while (props_len > 0) {
    err = decode_ed_prop(&ofp_prop, out, &props_len);
    if (err) {
        return err;
    }
    n_props++;
}
encap->n_props = n_props;
out->header = &encap->ofpact;
ofpact_finish_ENCAP(out, &encap);
```

The issue: `decode_ed_prop` calls `ofpbuf_put_uninit(out, ...)` which may reallocate `out->data`. After that, `encap` pointer (which points into `out->data`) becomes stale (use-after-free). Then `encap->n_props = n_props;` writes to the freed memory.

Wait, but `decode_ed_prop` has `out OVS_UNUSED` parameter... Let me re-check. Actually looking at the code in `ofp-ed-props.c`, `decode_ed_prop` does use `out`:

```c
struct ofpact_ed_prop_nsh_md_type *pnmt =
        ofpbuf_put_uninit(out, sizeof(*pnmt));
```

So `ofpbuf_put_uninit` may reallocate the buffer, invalidating `encap`. Then back in `decode_NXAST_RAW_ENCAP`, `encap->n_props = n_props;` writes to freed memory. That's the use-after-free WRITE.

